{"surah": 112, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ ", "translation": "Say, \"He is Allāh, [who is] One,[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَحَدٌ", "translit": "ٌdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "ٱللَّهُ ٱلصَّمَدُ ", "translation": "Allāh, the Eternal Refuge.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلصَّمَدُ", "translit": "ُdَmَّṣlٱ", "root": "صمد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ ", "translation": "He neither begets nor is born,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَلِدْ", "translit": "ْdِlَy", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُولَدْ", "translit": "ْdَlwُy", "root": "ولد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌۢ ", "translation": "Nor is there to Him any equivalent.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَكُن", "translit": "nُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُفُوًا", "translit": "āًwُfُk", "root": "كفا"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَحَدٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "فتارة يتعرف إليهم بالتقديس فيقول: \" قل هو الله أحد الله الصمد لم يلد ولم يولد ولم يكن له كفواً أحد \" وتارة يتعرف إليهم بصفات جلاله فيقول: \" الملك القدوس السلام المؤمن المهيمن العزيز الجبار المتكبر \" وتارة يتعرف إليهم في أفعاله المخوفة والمرجوة فيتلو عليهم سنته في أعدائه وفي أنبيائه فيقول: \" ألم تر كيف فعل ربك بعاد إرم ذات العماد - ألم تر كيف فعل ربك بأصحاب الفيل \". ولا يعدو القرآن هذه الأقسام الثلاثة وهي: الإرشاد إلى معرفة ذات الله وتقديسه، أو معرفة صفاته وأسمائه، أو معرفة أفعاله وسنته مع عباده،", "english": "And sometimes He makes Himself known to them through Sanctification (Taqdis) and says: \"Say: He is Allah, the One, Allah, the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is born, Nor is there to Him any equivalent.\" And sometimes He makes Himself known to them through the attributes of His Majesty and says: \"The Sovereign, the Holy, the Peace, the Faithful, the Guardian, the Exalted in Might, the Compeller, the Superior.\" And sometimes He makes Himself known to them in His fearful and hoped-for actions, and He recites to them His established way (Sunnah) with His enemies and with His prophets, and says: \"Have you not considered how your Lord dealt with 'Aad - [With] Iram - who had lofty pillars, The likes of whom had never been created in the land? And [with] Thamud, who carved out the rocks in the valley? And [with] Pharaoh, owner of the stakes? - [All of] whom oppressed within the lands And increased therein the corruption. So your Lord poured upon them a scourge of punishment. Indeed, your Lord is in observation.\" And the Quran does not exceed these three divisions, which are: Guidance to the knowledge of the Essence (Dhat) of Allah and His Sanctification (Taqdis), or knowledge of His attributes and His names, or knowledge of His actions and His established way (Sunnah) with His servants. And since Surah Al-Ikhlas contains one of these three divisions, which is Sanctification (Taqdis), the Messenger of Allah ﷺ equated it with a third of the Quran and said: \"Whoever recites Surah Al-Ikhlas has recited a third of the Quran\"[51] because the ultimate Sanctification (Taqdis) is to be One in three matters: He does not have from Him one who is His equal and likeness, and His statement \"He neither begets\" indicates this; and He is not from one who is His equal and likeness, and His statement \"nor is born\" indicates this; and there is not in His rank, even if not being an origin for Him nor a branch, one who is like Him, and His statement: \"Nor is there to Him any equivalent\" indicates this. And all of that is gathered by His statement, the Exalted: \"Say: He is Allah, the One\" and its totality is the elaboration of the statement \"There is no deity except Allah.\" These are the secrets of the Quran, and the likes of these secrets do not end.", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book6.doc.txt", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ولما اشتملت سورة الإخلاص على أحد هذه الأقسام الثلاثة وهو التقديس وازنها رسول الله ﭬ بثلث القرآن فقال: \"من قرأ سورة الإخلاص فقد قرأ ثلث القرآن\"[51] لأن منتهى التقديس أن يكون واحداً في ثلاثة أمور، لا يكون حاصلاً منه من هو نظيره وشبهه، ودل عليه قوله \" لم يلد \" ولا يكون حاصلاً ممن هو نظيره وشبهه، ودل عليه قوله \" ولم يولد \" ولا يكون في درجته وإن لم يكن أصلاً له ولا فرعاً من هو مثله، ودل عليه قوله: \" ولم يكن له كفواً أحد \" ويجمع جميع ذلك قوله تعالى: \" قل هو الله أحد \" وجملته تفصيل قول \" لا إله إلا الل", "english": "And since Surah Al-Ikhlas contains one of these three divisions, which is Sanctification (Taqdis), the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) equated it with one-third of the Quran, saying: 'Whoever recites Surah Al-Ikhlas has recited one-third of the Quran' [51] because the ultimate Sanctification (Taqdis) is to be One (Ahad) in three matters: there is no one who is His equal and likeness originating from Him, and this is indicated by His saying 'He begets not'; and there is no one who is His equal and likeness originating from whom He originates, and this is indicated by His saying 'nor is He begotten'; and there is no one who is His equal in rank, even if not being an origin for Him nor a branch, and this is indicated by His saying: 'And there is none comparable to Him.' And all of that is gathered by His saying, the Exalted: 'Say: He is Allah, the One (Ahad).' And its entirety is the elaboration of the statement 'There is no god but Allah.' These are the secrets (Asrar) of the Quran, and the likes of these secrets in the Quran are endless, 'nor is there anything fresh or dry but is in a Clear Book.' Therefore, Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Illuminate the Quran and seek its wonders, for in it is the knowledge of the former and the latter, and it is as he said. And none knows it except one who has prolonged in pondering its individual words, and his intellect (Fikr) has become pure for his understanding (Fahm) of it, until every word of it testifies to him that it is the speech of a Mighty (Jabbar), Subduing (Qahir), Sovereign (Malik), Powerful (Qadir), and that it is beyond the limit of human capability.", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book6.doc.txt", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقال ﭬ: (من صلى المغرب في جماعة ثم صلى بعدها ركعتين ولم يتكلم بشيء فيما بين ذلك من أمر الدنيا ويقرأ في الركعة الأولى فاتحة الكتاب وعشر آيات من أول سورة البقرة وآيتين من وسطها وإلهكم إله واحد لا إله إلا هو الرحمن الرحيم إن في خلق السموات والأرض إلى آخر الآية وقل هو الله أحد خمس عشرة مرة ثم يركع ويسجد فإذا قام في الركعة الثانية قرأ فاتحة الكتاب وآية الكرسي وآيتين بعدها إلى قوله أولئك أصحاب النار هم فيها خالدون وثلاث آيات من آخر سورة البقرة من قوله لله ما في السموات وما في الأرض إلى آخرها وقل هو ال", "english": "And he (the Prophet) said: 'Whoever prays Maghrib in congregation then prays after it two rak'ahs and does not speak anything between them from worldly matters, and recites in the first rak'ah: The Opening (Al-Fatihah) of the Book and ten verses from the beginning of Surah Al-Baqarah and two verses from its middle: \"And your God is One God, there is no god but He, the Most Merciful (Ar-Rahman), the Most Compassionate (Ar-Rahim)\" and \"Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth\" to the end of the verse, and \"Say: He is Allah, the One (Ahad)\" fifteen times, then bows and prostrates. When he stands in the second rak'ah, he recites: The Opening (Al-Fatihah) of the Book and the Throne Verse (Ayat al-Kursi) and two verses after it to His saying: \"Those are the companions of the Fire; they will abide therein eternally\" and three verses from the end of Surah Al-Baqarah from His saying: \"To Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth\" to its end, and \"Say: He is Allah, the One (Ahad)\" fifteen times.' He described from its reward in the hadith what exceeds enumeration.", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-10.doc.txt", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the diseases of the heart and their cure, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "فقد روى عن وهيب بن الورد أنه قال إن من الدعاء الذي لا يرد أن يصلي العبد ثنتي عشرة ركعة يقرأ في كل ركعة بأم الكتاب وآية الكرسي وقل هو الله أحد فإذا فرغ خر ساجدا ثم قال سبحان الذي لبس العز وقال به سبحان الذي تعطف بالمجد وتكرم به سبحان الذي أحصى كل شيء بعلمه سبحان الذي لا ينبغي التسبيح إلا له سبحان ذي المن والفضل سبحان ذي العز والكرم سبحان ذي الطول أسألك بمعاقد العز من عرشك ومنتهى الرحمة من كتابك وباسمك الأعظم وجدك الأعلى وكلماتك التامات العامات التي لا يجاوزهن بر ولا فاجر أن تصلى على محمد وعلى آل ", "english": "It has been narrated from Wahib ibn al-Ward that he said: Among the supplication (Dua) that is not rejected is that the servant (Abd) prays twelve units (Rak'ah), reciting in each unit the Mother of the Book (Umm al-Kitab) and the Throne Verse (Ayat al-Kursi) and 'Say: He is Allah, the One (Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad).' When he finishes, he falls prostrate (Sajda) and then says: 'Glory be to the One who wears majesty and says with it. Glory be to the One who wraps Himself in glory and honors with it. Glory be to the One who enumerates everything with His knowledge. Glory be to the One to whom glorification is not fitting except for Him. Glory be to the Possessor of favor and grace. Glory be to the Possessor of might and generosity. Glory be to the Possessor of abundance. I ask You by the bonds of might from Your Throne (Arsh) and the utmost of mercy from Your Book, and by Your Greatest Name (Ism al-A'zam) and Your Highest Majesty (Jadd al-A'la) and Your perfect, comprehensive words that no righteous or wicked one surpasses, that You send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad.' Then he asks for his need that contains no disobedience, and he is answered, if Allah the Exalted wills.", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-4.doc.txt", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the virtue and practice of remembering God, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Dhikr"}, {"arabic": "التاسع أن يراعى في قدوة الجمعة ما ذكرناه في غيرها فإذا سمع قراءة الإمام لم يقرأ سوى الفاتحة فإذا فرغ من الجمعة قرأ الحمد لله سبع مرات قبل أن يتكلم وقل هو الله أحد والمعوذتين سبعا سبعا وروى بعض السلف: أن من فعله عصم من الجمعة إلى الجمعة وكان حرزا له من الشيطان ويستحب أن يقول بعد الجمعة: اللهم يا غني يا حميد يا مبدىء يا معيد يا رحيم يا ودود أغنني بحلالك عن حرامك وبفضلك عمن سواك. يقال من داوم على هذا الدعاء أغناه الله سبحانه عن خلقه ورزقه من حيث لا يحتسب ثم يصلي بعد الجمعة ست ركعات.", "english": "Ninth: One should observe in the Friday prayer leader what we have mentioned in others. When he hears the Imam's recitation, he should not recite except the Opening (Al-Fatihah). When he finishes the Friday prayer, he should read 'Praise be to Allah' seven times before speaking, and 'Say: He is Allah, the One' (Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad) and the two seeking refuge surahs (Al-Mu'awwidhatayn) seven times each. Some of the predecessors (Salaf) narrated: Whoever does this is protected from one Friday to the next and it becomes a shield for him from Satan. It is recommended to say after the Friday prayer: O Allah, O Rich (Al-Ghani), O Praiseworthy (Al-Hamid), O Originator (Al-Mubdi'), O Restorer (Al-Mu'id), O Merciful (Al-Rahim), O Loving (Al-Wadud), enrich me with Your lawful provision over Your unlawful, and with Your bounty over whoever besides You. It is said: Whoever persists in this supplication, Allah the Exalted enriches him from His creation and provides for him from where he does not expect. Then he prays after the Friday prayer six units of prayer.", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-4.doc.txt", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications and their spiritual significance, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "3": [{"arabic": "رواه عن النبي ﭬ أنه قال: (إن الله تعالى يمجد نفسه كل يوم ويقول إني أنا الله رب العالمين إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا الحي القيوم إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا العلي العظيم إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا لم ألد ولم أولد إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا العفو الغفور إني أنا الله لا إله إلا أنا مبدىء كل شيء وإلي يعود العزيز الحكيم الرحمن الرحيم مالك يوم الدين خالق الخير والشر خالق الجنة والنار الواحد الأحد الفرد الصمد الذي لم يتخذ صاحبة ولا ولدا الفرد الوتر عالم الغيب والشهادة الملك القدوس السلام المؤمن المهي", "english": "It is narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he said: (Indeed, Allah the Exalted glorifies Himself every day and says: I am Allah, Lord of the worlds; I am Allah, there is no god but I, the Living, the Self-Sustaining; I am Allah, there is no god but I, the Most High, the Most Great; I am Allah, there is no god but I, I did not beget nor was I begotten; I am Allah, there is no god but I, the Pardoner, the Forgiving; I am Allah, there is no god but I, the Originator of every thing and to Me it returns, the Mighty, the Wise, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, Master of the Day of Judgment, Creator of good and evil, Creator of Paradise and Hell, the One, the Unique, the Single, the Eternal, who has not taken a companion nor a son, the Single, the Odd, Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the King, the Holy, the Peace, the Faithful, the Guardian, the Mighty, the Compeller, the Majestic, the Creator, the Maker, the Fashioner, the Great, the Exalted, the Powerful, the Subduer, the Forbearing, the Generous, Possessor of praise and glory, Knower of the secret and the more hidden, the Able, the Provider, above the creation and the creatures[135]). And He mentioned before every word 'I am Allah, there is no god but I' as we have brought it in the first. So whoever supplicates with these names, let him say: You are Allah, there is no god but You, such and such. So whoever supplicates with them is written among the prostrating, the humble ones who will be neighbors to Muhammad, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and the prophets (prayers of Allah be upon them) in the abode of majesty, and for him is the reward of the worshippers in the heavens and the earths. And may Allah bless Muhammad and every chosen servant.", "book_file": "vol1_Vol1-book-9.doc.txt", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications and their spiritual significance", "badge": "Du'a"}], "4": [{"arabic": "قرآن شيء إلا وهو هدى ونور وتعرف من الله تعالى إلى خلقه، فتارة يتعرف إليهم بالتقديس فيقول: \"قل هو الله أحد الله الصمد لم يلد ولم يولد ولم يكن له كفواً أحد\" وتارة يتعرف إليهم بصفات جلاله فيقول: \"الملك القدوس السلام المؤمن المهيمن العزيز الجبار المتكبر\" وتارة يتعرف إليهم في أفعاله المخوفة والمرجوة فيتلو عليهم سنته في أعدائه وفي أنبيائه فيقول: \"ألم تر كيف فعل ربك بعاد إرم ذات العماد - ألم تر كيف فعل ربك بأصحاب الفيل\". ولا يعدو القرآن هذه الأقسام الثلاثة وهي: الإرشاد إلى معرفة ذات الله وتقديسه، أو مع", "english": "The Quran is nothing except it is guidance and light and a making-known from Allah the Exalted to His creation, so at times He makes Himself known to them through sanctification (Taqdis) when He says: \"Say: He is Allah, the One, Allah, the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is born, nor is there to Him any equivalent (Kufuwan) one.\" And at times He makes Himself known to them through attributes of His majesty when He says: \"The Sovereign, the Holy, the Peace, the Faithful, the Guardian, the Exalted in Might, the Compeller, the Superior.\" And at times He makes Himself known to them in His actions that are feared and hoped for, and He recites to them His way (Sunnah) with His enemies and with His prophets when He says: \"Have you not considered how your Lord dealt with 'Aad - Iram of the pillars? Have you not considered how your Lord dealt with the companions of the elephant?\" And the Quran does not exceed these three divisions, which are: guidance to knowledge of the essence (Dhat) of Allah and His sanctification (Taqdis), or knowledge of His attributes and His names, or knowledge of His actions and His way (Sunnah) with His servants. And since Surah Al-Ikhlas contained one of these three divisions, which is sanctification (Taqdis), the Messenger of Allah ﷺ equated it with a third of the Quran, saying: \"Whoever recites Surah Al-Ikhlas has recited a third of the Quran\" because the utmost sanctification (Taqdis) is to be one in three matters: there is not originating from Him one who is His counterpart and likeness, and His statement \"He neither begets\" indicates this; and there is not originating from one who is His counterpart and likeness, and His statement \"nor is born\" indicates this; and there is not in His rank, even if not being an origin for Him nor a branch, one who is His like, and His statement indicates this: \"nor is there to Him any equivalent (Kufuwan) one.\" And all of that is gathered by His statement the Exalted: \"Say: He is Allah, the One\" and its entirety is the detailing of the saying \"There is no god but Allah.\" These are the secrets of the Quran, and examples of these secrets in the Quran do not end, \"and not a moist thing nor a dry thing except in a clear Book.\" And for that reason Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Illuminate the Quran and seek its wonders, for in it is the knowledge of the first and the last, and it is as he said, and none knows it except one who prolonged in the individual words his thought (Fikr) and purified for it his understanding until every word of it testifies for him that it is the speech of a Compeller, a Subduer, a Sovereign, a Powerful, and that it is outside the limit of human capability.", "book_file": "vol4_Vol4-book6.doc.txt", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's insights on the inner dimensions of faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 250, 726], [2, 726, 1246], [3, 1246, 2101], [4, 2101, 2816]], "2": [[1, 250, 1184], [2, 1184, 2503]], "3": [[1, 250, 644], [2, 644, 1382], [3, 1382, 2071], [4, 2071, 3104]], "4": [[1, 350, 1012], [2, 1012, 1674], [3, 1674, 2175], [4, 2175, 3139], [5, 3139, 3763]]}, "husary": {"1": [[1, 350, 1323], [2, 1323, 2384], [3, 2384, 4130], [4, 4130, 5589]], "2": [[1, 350, 2198], [2, 2198, 4806]], "3": [[1, 350, 1087], [2, 1087, 2469], [3, 2469, 3759], [4, 3759, 5694]], "4": [[1, 400, 1813], [2, 1813, 3226], [3, 3226, 4296], [4, 4296, 6355], [5, 6355, 7688]]}, "minshawi": {"1": [[1, 90, 602], [2, 602, 1370], [3, 1370, 2301], [4, 2301, 3070]], "2": [[1, 10, 1088], [2, 1088, 2580]], "3": [[1, 30, 386], [2, 386, 1223], [3, 1223, 2006], [4, 2006, 3270]], "4": [[1, 10, 956], [2, 956, 1902], [3, 1902, 2472], [4, 2472, 3999], [5, 3999, 4710]]}, "mah": [{"word": "بسم", "start": 0.031, "end": 0.754}, {"word": "الله", "start": 0.794, "end": 1.396}, {"word": "الرحمن", "start": 1.456, "end": 2.359}, {"word": "الرحيم", "start": 2.419, "end": 4.888}, {"word": "قل", "start": 5.009, "end": 5.31}, {"word": "هو", "start": 5.41, "end": 5.691}, {"word": "الله", "start": 5.751, "end": 6.694}, {"word": "أحد", "start": 6